  "content": {
    "id": "p130-task-13-18",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "True or False?",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p130-task-13-18-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The sum of two even functions is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Let $f$ and $g$ be even functions and let $h(x)=f(x)+g(x)$ on their common symmetric domain.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(-x)+g(-x)\\\\h(-x)&=f(x)+g(x)\\\\h(-x)&=h(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe sum is even, so the statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-13-18-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The sum of two odd functions is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Let $f$ and $g$ be odd functions and let $h(x)=f(x)+g(x)$ on their common symmetric domain.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(-x)+g(-x)\\\\h(-x)&=-f(x)-g(x)\\\\h(-x)&=-\\bigl(f(x)+g(x)\\bigr)\\\\h(-x)&=-h(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe sum is odd, so the statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-13-18-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The sum of an even and odd function is neither odd nor even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False as written.\n\nThe even function $f(x)=0$ and the odd function $g(x)=x$ have the odd sum $x$.",
          "worked": "Take $f(x)=0$ and $g(x)=x$ on $\\mathbb{R}$. The zero function is even, and $g$ is odd. Let $h(x)=f(x)+g(x)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(x)&=0+x\\\\h(x)&=x.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe sum is odd and is not even: $h(-1)=-1$, whereas $h(1)=1$. This counterexample makes the statement false as written.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-13-18-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "The difference of two even functions is neither even nor odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The difference is even.",
          "worked": "Let $f$ and $g$ be even functions and let $h(x)=f(x)-g(x)$ on their common symmetric domain.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(-x)-g(-x)\\\\h(-x)&=f(x)-g(x)\\\\h(-x)&=h(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe difference is even, so the statement is false.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-13-18-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "The difference of two odd functions is odd.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "Let $f$ and $g$ be odd functions and let $h(x)=f(x)-g(x)$ on their common symmetric domain.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(-x)-g(-x)\\\\h(-x)&=-f(x)+g(x)\\\\h(-x)&=-\\bigl(f(x)-g(x)\\bigr)\\\\h(-x)&=-h(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe difference is odd, so the statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p130-task-13-18-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "The quotient of an even and odd function is even.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The quotient is odd wherever defined.",
          "worked": "Let $f$ be even and $g$ be odd. Define $h(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}$ on their common symmetric domain, excluding points where $g(x)=0$. Since $g$ is odd, this remaining domain is symmetric.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=\\frac{f(-x)}{g(-x)}\\\\h(-x)&=\\frac{f(x)}{-g(x)}\\\\h(-x)&=-h(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe quotient is odd. In particular, $f(x)=1$ and $g(x)=x$ give $h(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x}$ for $x\\ne0$, which is not even. Therefore the statement is false.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
